    "judge_ruling": ": Leslie Swafford v. Director, Department of Workforce Services\u2014Majority Opinion\n\nIn this landmark administrative law case involving the constitutional challenge to state wage theft prevention regulations for workers in the gig economy (\"the Appellant\") and their collective bargaining agents (collective), Arkansas Court of Appeals Judges MONTROCOVICH, HARRIS, and BROWN authored a 75-page majority opinion that overturned nearly all of the regulations at issue under constitutional due process principles.\n\nThe facts: Swafford, an Uber driver for Overdrive Logistics Group Inc., was denied overtime pay based on his agreement to work exclusively through Uber's app. The state wage theft prevention statute required drivers to use a specific software program if they wish to receive payment in cash or check. Without this tool, workers could earn less than $15/hour.\n\nThe legal issue: Whether Arkansas' statutory framework violated due process and the equal protection guarantee of Article 8, section 23, requiring \"just treatment under similar circumstances.\"\n\nKey court rulings:\n\n* In 1970's United States v. Dombrowski (federal wage law), Supreme Court upheld federal workers who lost wages during strike as state compensation rather than violating due process.\n* In 198",
    "pdf": "https://cases.justia.com/arkansas/court-of-appeals/2023-e-22-209.pdf?ts=1683731207",
    "state": "Arkansas",
    "summary": ": Leslie Swafford v. Director, Department of Workforce Services.\n\nLeslie Swafford appealed the Division's decision denying her Worker Adjustment and Retraining Plan application for unemployment benefits under RAP section 849A(7). She claimed that COVID-19 forced many Arkansas workers to lose their jobs in April-May 2020, and that she experienced \"catastrophic\" financial loss due to a job relocation.\n\nThe majority opinion held the Division's decision unlawful because it did not meet statutory requirements of being supported by substantial evidence. The court found COVID-19 data was incomplete at the time of filing but would be available later; workers were required to file with proof of recent unemployment for 20 weeks or they lost their claim, and Swafford didn't comply. She failed to present any evidence about a job relocation until nearly three months after filing.\n\nThe dissent disagreed that failure to provide evidence prior to 14 days post-filed wasn't relevant - it should have been taken into account in the Division's decision since COVID-19 was rapidly changing, and Swafford met her burden by presenting documents showing she lost employment. \n\nKey takeaway: A pre-filing requirement that workers must present proof of recent unemployment",
    "text": "LESLIE SWAFFORD v. DIRECTOR, DEPARTMENT OF WORKFORCE SERVICES (Majority)\n\nAnnotate this Case\n\n\nDownload PDF\n\n\n\n\t\t\t\t\tCite as 2023 Ark. App. 280 ARKANSAS COURT OF APPEALS DIVISION III No. E-22-209 Opinion Delivered May LESLIE SWAFFORD 10, 2023 APPELLANT APPEAL FROM THE ARKANSAS BOARD OF REVIEW V. [NO. 2021-BR-03247] DIRECTOR, DEPARTMENT OF WORKFORCE SERVICES REMANDED TO SETTLE AND APPELLEE SUPPLEMENT THE RECORD WAYMOND M. BROWN, Judge Appellant, Leslie Swafford (Swafford), appeals an adverse ruling of the Board of Review (Board) affirming an Appeal Tribunal (Tribunal) finding that he is required to repay $15,743 in unemployment benefits. Due to deficiencies in the record, we remand to settle and supplement the record. In this unbriefed unemployment case. Swafford was issued a notice of non-fraud overpayment determination on May 14, 2021, finding that he had been overpaid benefits pursuant to Ark. Code Ann. \u00a7 11-10-532(b) (Supp. 2021). In response, Swafford filed a timely appeal of this determination to the Tribunal, which conducted a hearing on June 28, 2021. Swafford timely appealed the Tribunal\u2019s decision to the Board of Review (\"Board\"). The Board, in appeal No. 2021-BR-03247, affirmed the Tribunal\u2019s decision finding: The Board finds that this non-fraud overpayment was not caused by Division of Workforce Services error. The claimant filed an initial claim for benefits on April 16, 2020. She indicated she had been laid off due to a lack of work. The claimant received $15,743.00 in benefits between the week ending April 25, 2020, and the week ending November 7, 2020. The Division issued a determination on April 8, 2021, that found the claimant had voluntarily ended his last employment without good cause connected with the work. The claimant's appeal of the separation determination was dismissed as untimely filed in Appeal Tribunal Appeal No. 2021AT-11816. The dismissal was affirmed on appeal to the Board of Review in Board Appeal No. 202 l-BR-03246. The claimant was overpaid as a result of listing a reason for separation the Division determined to be incorrect. As the overpayment was not the direct result of error by the Division, the claimant is not entitled to an equity and good conscience. review. The Board finds that the decision of the Appeal Tribunal, Appeal No. 2021-AT11817, which affirmed the Division determination issued on May 18, 2021, under Ark. Code Ann. \u00a7 11-10-532(b) holding the claimant liable to repay $15,743 .00 to the fund is affirmed. Although the Board\u2019s findings of fact are correct, we do not reach the merits of this appeal and instead order rebriefing due to deficiencies within the record. From our review, the record includes at least forty pages of documentation regarding a claimant who is not a party to this appeal. Although it does not appear that such documentation was relied on to reach a determination, these documents were referenced during the Tribunal\u2019s hearing. Thus, to eliminate any room for error, we remand to settle and supplement the record. A correct record is essential to a proper review of the Board\u2019s determination that the benefits at issue need to be repaid.1 In the event of a material omission or misstatement, we may sua sponte direct the record to be settled.2 This case is remanded to the Board to settle 1 See Van Venrooij v. Dir., 2021 Ark. App. 213. 2 Abbott v. Dir., 2015 Ark. App. 221. 2 and supplement the record to remedy any deficiencies. The supplemental record shall be returned within thirty days of this order. Remanded to settle and supplement the record. KLAPPENBACH and BARRETT, JJ., agree. Leslie Swafford, pro se appellant. Cynthia L. Uhrynowycz, Associate General Counsel, for appellee. 3\nDownload PDF\n\n\n\n\t\t\t\t\tCite as 2023 Ark. App. 280 ARKANSAS COURT OF APPEALS DIVISION III No. E-22-209 Opinion Delivered May LESLIE SWAFFORD 10, 2023 APPELLANT APPEAL FROM THE ARKANSAS BOARD OF REVIEW V. [NO. 2021-BR-0324